      id: 'pm.devops.trunk',
      title: 'Continuous integration and trunk-based development',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'Two developers each work six weeks on their own copy of the code and then combine them. What often happens?',
          options: ['Combining goes by itself, version control handles it', 'They changed the same parts differently and spend days untangling', 'Tests become unnecessary, since each tested his own part', 'They only have to pick the newest copy and discard the other'],
          answer: 'They changed the same parts differently and spend days untangling',
          why: 'The longer apart, the further the copies drift. That is called integration pain.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'In version control a developer often works on a branch: a separate copy of the code, so he can build without disturbing others. The main line where everything comes together is called the trunk or main.\n\nThe longer a branch stays apart, the further it drifts from the trunk. When combining (merging), merge conflicts arise: two people changed the same lines differently, and someone has to work out by hand what is right.',
        'Continuous integration (CI) is the answer: everyone merges their work into the trunk at least daily, and every merge runs the build and the automated tests. That way you find a conflict or fault within minutes, while you still remember what you did.\n\nThe CI in CI/CD is therefore not a tool but a habit. A team with a CI server that only merges after weeks is not doing continuous integration.',
        'Trunk-based development goes a step further: branches live a few hours to a day at most. DORA, the research programme behind the State of DevOps reports, lists as signs: three or fewer active branches, merging at least daily, and no code freeze (a period in which nobody may add code).\n\nThe opposite is feature branches that live for weeks, followed by an integration phase. DORA links those to lower performance.',
        'How is that possible when a feature is weeks of work? By cutting the work into small pieces that can each go safely into the trunk, even though the feature isn’t finished. The unfinished part is switched off, with a switch we cover later.\n\nFor Lean people: a long branch is work in progress. It delivers nothing, ages, and hides faults until the merge. Integrating daily is making small batches.',
        'What teams argue about: working with pull requests, where a colleague reads every change before it may enter the trunk. That is good for quality, but waiting for a review can take hours or days. Many teams therefore keep pull requests small and review within a few hours.\n\nA sharp question: “How old is your oldest open branch?” Weeks old means the integration pain is still to come.',
      ],
